<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="folate transformations II (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3841" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; This pathway describes the formation of the formyl and methyl derivatives of tetrahydrofolate (vitamin B9) |CITS: [9190084][10785666]|. Folates are involved in a wide range of key metabolic functions in plants |CITS: [11960743][12644692]|, mediating fluxes through C1-pathways with a high demand for methylated compounds such as secondary metabolites |CITS: [11337394]|. Plants prefer the polyglutamylated forms of folates (compare |FRAME:PWY-2161|, |FRAME:PWY-2161B|) since the turnover rate of those compounds is markedly increased |CITS: [9190084][Scott00]| and meets the high demands for folates as observed in plants |CITS: [11960743]|. In addition the polyglutamylated forms of folate facilitate the retention of the vitamin within the cell and its subcellular compartments |CITS: [1916088]|. The plant enzymes involved in this pathway, although essentially catalyzing the same steps, have been found to differ in many regards from their bacterial counterparts |CITS: [9190084][14745019][15500462]|. Folates are tripartite molecules and are made up of pterin, p-aminobenzoate (PABA) and glutamate moieties. The one-carbon units are either attached to the N-5 of the pterin moietie, to the N-10 of the PABA moiety or are brigded in between those two (as in 5,10-methenyl or methylene-THF) |CITS: [Basset05]|. The different forms of folates are easily converted into each other through a tight network of reactions (|FRAME:PWY-2201|). Most of the enzymes have been identified in plants but some of them, such as the formyltetrahydrofolate deformylase, presumably involved in the mutual conversion of tetrahydrofolate and its 10-formyl derivative, remain to be demonstrated. Among the folates |FRAME:N5-Formyl-THF-Glu-N &quot;&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt;-formyl-tetrahydrofolate&quot;| is the most enigmatic. Even though |FRAME:N5-Formyl-THF-Glu-N &quot;&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt;-formyl-tetrahydrofolate&quot;| is the only folate derivative that does not serve as a cofactor in C1 metabolism, it is the most common and stable form of folates found in plants |CITS: [8480361]|. Moreover, |FRAME:N5-Formyl-THF-Glu-N &quot;&lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt;-formyl-tetrahydrofolate&quot;| is known to inhibit most of the folate-dependent enzymes at physiological concentrations. The biological role of this compound is still poorly understood, although it has been proposed to participate in the regulation of essential biosynthetic steps such as the formation of serine during photorespiration |CITS: [15888445][12207015]|. The complete set of folate enzymes is only present in mitochondria. However, the recent discovery that folylpolyglutamate synthases are present in cytosol, mitochondria and plastids with each of them encoded by a different gene in Arabidopsis thaliana |CITS: [11752472]| points to the fact that at least parts of the pathway can be carried out independently in those compartments. Interestingly, the enzyme hydrolyzing the polyglutamylated folates (&amp;gamma;-glutamyl hydrolase) has been found to be an extracellular enzyme in plants |CITS: [8912628]|. Further understanding of the transport and conversions of folates and their derivatives within the different cell compartments, and their regulation, is required before attempting to genetically engineer this pathway.</Description>
