    <DataNode elementId="AT1G50480-MONOMER" textLabel="formate-tetrahydrofolate ligase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SPK5" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="695" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The spinach 10-formyl-THF synthetase sequence |CITS: [1644815]| was used to screen the Arabidopsis dbEST to select an N-terminal sequence homolog to the spinach sequence. Finally a cDNA was isolated containing an amino acid sequence conserved in other 10-formyl-THF synthetases |CITS: [Skavdahl99]|. Unlike in other eukaryotes, where a trifunctional enzyme (C1-THF-synthase) converts 10-formyl-THF to 5,10-methylene-THF, plants have been shown to possess a monofunctional 10-formyl-THF synthetase. Furthermore, it has been demonstrated that the C1-THF synthase/SHMT pathway (which includes the 10-formyl-THF synthetase) in Arabidopsis can compensate for deficiency of serine when the primary glycine decarboxylase complex/serine hydroxymethyltransferase (GDC/SHMT) pathway is disturbed |CITS: [12668769]|. The 10-formyl-THF synthetase isolated from pea exhibited a strong preference for polyglutamylated substrates. The affinity increased significantly from mono (Km = 40 uM) to pentaglutamylated THF (Km = 3 uM) |CITS: [Kirk94]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G50480-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G50480.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SPK5" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="AAD56290" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-9910, RXN-9323" />
